Earrings of baked polymer clay with stainless steel plated in gold findings fall under heading 7117 (imitation jewelry). Subheading 7117.19 covers imitation jewelry of base metal, but the clay is the main component, so 7117.90 applies (other). The statistical suffix for 7117.90.9000 is "Other". Earrings made of polymer clay, a plastic, would not be classified under 7117.19 because that subheading covers base metal jewelry, whether or not plated with precious metal. Under 7117.90, 7117.90.55 covers jewelry valued over $0.33 per dozen pieces, and 7117.90.75 covers "other." CBP rulings on polymer clay earrings typically classify them in 7117.90.7500, which is the "Other" category in 7117.90.

The structure of 7117.90 is: 7117.90.10 (cost under $0.33 per dozen pieces, 7117.90.10), 7117.90.30 (cost over...), 7117.90.40 (of plastics), 7117.90.50, 7117.90.55 (of plastics) 7117.90.75 (other). Sorting through these: 7117.90.55 is "Other" under "Other: Valued not over 20 cents each," while 7117.90.75 is "Other." Because the earrings are not valued under $0.20 each, and are not of precious metal, 7117.90.75 is the choice.

{"hts_code": "7117.90.7500", "reason": "Polymer clay earrings with gold-plated stainless steel findings are imitation jewelry of heading 7117 that is not of base metal as its main material, so they fall under the residual subheading 7117.90.75."}
